// Provenance of oracle.dll -- the external oracle for the PE cursor/icon group height convention.
//
// oracle-cursor.cur and oracle-icon.ico are synthetic 1bpp images, 128x128 and 32x32, whose own
// file directories state the DISPLAY height (128 and 32) and whose DIBs state twice that
// (biHeight 256 and 64), because a cursor or icon DIB stacks its XOR bitmap on top of its AND
// mask. oracle.dll is what the Microsoft toolchain makes of them:
//
//   rc.exe   /nologo /fo oracle.res oracle.rc
//   link.exe /nologo /DLL /NOENTRY /MACHINE:X64 /OUT:oracle.dll oracle.res
//
//   rc.exe   Windows SDK 10.0.26100.0 (Resource Compiler 10.0.10011.16384)
//   link.exe Microsoft Incremental Linker 14.51.36248.0 (MSVC 14.51.36231)
//
// What it emits, and the whole point of keeping it:
//
//   RT_GROUP_CURSOR entries  dirW=128 dirH=256   dirW=32 dirH=64    <- height DOUBLED
//   RT_GROUP_ICON   entries  dirW=128 dirH=128   dirW=32 dirH=32    <- height NOT doubled
//
// One tool run, one pair of geometries, both conventions side by side. The same doubling appears
// in the cursor groups of shipped Windows binaries (user32.dll, comctl32.dll), which no rc.exe
// run of ours produced.
//
// Note rc.exe refuses a PNG-payload cursor outright ("error RC2176: old DIB ... pass it through
// SDKPAINT"), so there is no Microsoft convention for one and the doubling is unconditional.

1 CURSOR "oracle-cursor.cur"
1 ICON "oracle-icon.ico"
